# Agent instructions: build this map package in ArcGIS Pro

You are on a Windows computer with ArcGIS Pro 3.3 or later. The package was made on a computer without ArcGIS Pro, so the native project does not exist yet. Build it, compare it with the reference figures, and report.

A person who only wants the short version: `arcgis/README.md` has a four-step quick start. This file is the full procedure with the acceptance checks.

**Goal:** an editable ArcGIS Pro project (`{{ p.id }}_Maps.aprx`) with one map and one layout per figure ({{ maps|length }} figures{% if atlases %}, {{ atlases|length }} map book{{ "" if atlases|length == 1 else "s" }}{% endif %}), its file geodatabase, layer files, and PDF, PNG and AIX exports, plus a written report.

## 1. Set up

1. Put the package at a short local path: `C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}` (the folder that holds `spec`, `data`, `maps`, `arcgis`). If you were given a ZIP, extract it there. Do not work in OneDrive, Dropbox or a network share.
2. Install the fonts: open `C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}\fonts\inter`, select all `.otf` files, right-click, **Install**. If you cannot install fonts, continue; the build warns and the text falls back to another font.
3. Use ArcGIS Pro's own Python. Do not install packages, clone the environment or ask for administrator rights. The builder imports only ArcPy and the standard library.
4. Optional check that needs no ArcPy: `python C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}\arcgis\build_arcgis_pro.py --preflight` should print `"errors": []`.

About the data: `docs/data_dictionary.md` lists every layer, its fields, and whether it was clipped or filtered for this project (a clipped layer is not the publisher's full dataset). `docs/data_sources.md` has the source URLs for refreshing a layer.

## 2. Build

Pick one route. Each run writes a new folder `arcgis\native\build_<date>_<time>\` and never changes the project that is open.

**A. Toolbox (simplest).** Start ArcGIS Pro, create a new project from the **Catalog** template (any name, any scratch folder). In the Catalog pane: Toolboxes > Add Toolbox > `C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}\arcgis\Build_Maps.pyt`. Run **Build project and layouts** with the defaults.

**B. Python window.** In ArcGIS Pro: View > Python window, then paste (one line):

```python
exec(open(r"C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}\arcgis\build_arcgis_pro.py").read()); print(build(r"C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}"))
```

**C. Command line, no ArcGIS Pro window.** Pro 3.7 or later:

```bat
"C:\Program Files\ArcGIS\Pro\bin\Python\Scripts\propy.bat" C:\GIS\{{ p.id }}\arcgis\build_arcgis_pro.py --root C:\GIS\{{ p.id }} --template NEW
```

On Pro 3.3 to 3.6 a stand-alone script cannot create a project, so save any blank project first (for example `C:\GIS\Blank\Blank.aprx`) and pass `--template C:\GIS\Blank\Blank.aprx`.

Expect about one minute per figure. Leave ArcGIS Pro alone until it finishes.

## 3. Done means

Check each item. All must hold before you call the build finished.

- [ ] The run folder has `native_build_report.json` with `"status": "BUILT; visual review pending"` and has **no** `BUILD_INCOMPLETE.txt`.
- [ ] `{{ p.id }}_Maps.aprx` opens. It has {{ maps|length + atlases|length }} layouts and no layer with a red exclamation mark. On first open use **Save Project As** into your own folder.
- [ ] `exports\` holds a PDF for each of the {{ maps|length + atlases|length }} layouts and a PNG and AIX for each of the {{ maps|length }} figures.
- [ ] Each exported PDF matches its reference in `maps\pdf\` (open both side by side). Use the checklist in section 4 for every figure.
- [ ] `"font_installed": true` in the report, or you state plainly that the font was substituted.
- [ ] Every entry under `"warnings"` in the report is listed in your written report with what it affects.

